import Sys
import Text
import Functor
import Word
import WordArray
import Intern
import Scry.CstStore
import Json
import Scry.Identity
import Scry.QuireFindings
import Scry.Lower
/-!
# Scry.ProjectCache: the per-translation-unit parse and lowering caches

The project driver's expensive step is tree-sitter parsing plus the native-store copy
(`Scry.Native.parse` → `Scry.CstStore`); `cc -E` and lowering are comparatively cheap.
This module caches the **`scry-cst 1` store** per translation unit, keyed by the source
path, its text and the preprocessing flags, and validates the entry against a digest of
the *actual preprocessed text*. That second check is what makes the cache safe when a
header or a macro changes: a stale entry does not match the digest and is a miss, even if
the outer key did not change. A stale hit therefore cannot silently reuse a tree from
different code.

The second cache is the **per-TU lowering** (`Stage B` of
`wiz-notes/incremental-scry.md` §6.2): the `Scry.TU` a CST lowers to, keyed by the
inputs lowering actually reads — the full CST digest, the lowering/version identity, the
preprocessing digest, the unit target, and the original file paths and text digests
(origins and warnings carry original positions, which come from the line map and texts,
not from the preprocessed text). It is opt-in through the same `Scry.CacheOpts` as the
parse cache and stores exactly one record per TU, written atomically. See
"The lowering cache" below.

The cache is a separate directory (`--cache[=DIR]`, default `.scry-project-cache`). Each
entry is `DIR/<key>.tu`, written through a sibling `.tmp` and renamed. A malformed or
truncated entry is `corrupt`; an entry from another format or frontend, or one whose
key/pre-digest does not match, is a `miss`. Both are recomputed and rewritten, so a bad
entry costs a parse, never a wrong result.

The store format (`native/store.c`, `scry-cst 1`) is unchanged. This cache stores the
same text the `scry cst` command writes; it does not add a second serialisation of the
CST.

## What an entry depends on

An entry is reused only when every one of these agrees:

| input | where it is checked |
|---|---|
| the resolved source path, the spelling interned in the CST's path table, and the source text | the outer key |
| the unit's preprocessing flags, in order | the outer key |
| `--allow-errors` and `--keep-system-headers` | the outer key (strictness, and which text the store was built from) |
| the preprocessed text (headers, macros, `-I`, the environment `cc` saw) | the `pre` digest, recomputed from the actual `cc -E` output |
| the entry format | the first line (`Scry.projectCacheMagic`); another format is a **miss**, not corruption |
| the frontend that produced the CST (tree-sitter runtime/grammar, the shim, `Scry.Native`, the `scry-cst` spill, preprocessing/stripping policy) | the `frontend` line (`Scry.projectCacheFrontend`); another frontend is a **miss** |
| **every CST column** (including `nextSib` and the child CSR that the spill does not write) | the `digest` line: `Scry.cstFullDigest` of the store on save, recomputed on load; a mismatch is `corrupt` and the entry is rewritten |
| the CST body | `scry-cst 1`'s magic, section counts, row widths and the stored byte length |

A rule pack is deliberately **not** keyed: it cannot change the cached CST, and findings
are recomputed from the CST on every run, so a warm cache with a changed pack produces
the new findings (pinned by `scry/tests/project.wiz`). Points-to and the rule engine are
likewise rerun rather than cached. Lowering is cached separately (the next section), under
its own key, because it reads the original texts and the full CST digest rather than the
parse key's inputs.

## The lowering cache

`Scry.lowerCacheSave`/`Scry.lowerCacheLoad` store one TU per record at
`DIR/lower-<key>.tu` whenever `Scry.CacheOpts.enabled` is set (the same opt-in as the
parse cache; there is no second flag). An entry is the unit's `Scry.TU` — every
function/block/statement/terminator with its span and register types, the warnings in
order, and the external calls — plus the parse error count, written as one canonical
JSON record per line. It contains no project-global id: the merge
(`Scry.projectOfUnitsWith`) assigns file/function/site ids in unit order on every run, so
a cached TU is safe at any project position.

The key names every input the lowering reads:

| input | why |
|---|---|
| the entry format and `Scry.projectLowererVersion` | a writer or lowering change is a clean miss |
| `Scry.cstFullDigest` | the exact CST the lowering consumed (all columns, including the ones the spill does not write) |
| `Scry.lowerPreDigest pre` | headers and macros (`cc -E` output) **and the line map**: a same-length `#line` rewrite blanks to the same text but changes every origin |
| the unit target (`Scry.TU.path`) | two units with identical text at different paths must not share a TU |
| every original file path and text digest | every `Wil.Span` and warning carries an original file/line/column from the line map and texts, not from the preprocessed text (H5) |
| `--allow-errors` | explicit, alongside the CST digest that already distinguishes a recovery tree |

On load the header names the key inputs and the body's length and 64-bit digest; a key
or version mismatch is a `miss`, and a truncated body, a digest mismatch, a malformed
line or a body that does not re-render identically is `corrupt`. Both are recomputed and
rewritten, so corruption costs a lowering, never a stale TU. The write is one atomic
record (a sibling `.tmp` and a rename, through `Scry.cacheWriteAtomic`).

The frontend stamp is a constant, not a hash of the grammar sources: an installed
toolchain has no source path to hash, and hashing a 30 MB `parser.c` per translation unit
would cost more than the parse it protects. Bumping the constant is the contract; the
golden pins it.
-/

/-- The findings index's storage backend (see `Scry.QuireFindings`). `json` is the
default: the document a reader can inspect, and the format the driver had before the
index existed. `quire` is the page-backed store (`--cache-backend=quire`). -/
inductive Scry.CacheBackend where
  | json
  | quire

def Scry.CacheBackend.show : Scry.CacheBackend → String
  | .json => "json"
  | .quire => "quire"

def Scry.CacheBackend.ofName (s : String) : Option Scry.CacheBackend :=
  match String.eq s "json" with
  | .true => .some .json
  | .false => match String.eq s "quire" with
    | .true => .some .quire
    | .false => .none

/-- The file extension of a findings index. -/
def Scry.CacheBackend.ext : Scry.CacheBackend → String
  | .json => "json"
  | .quire => "quire"

/-- Cache policy: off, or a directory and a findings-index backend. `--cache-stats`
implies enabled. -/
structure Scry.CacheOpts where
  enabled : Bool
  dir : String
  backend : Scry.CacheBackend

def Scry.CacheOpts.none : Scry.CacheOpts :=
  Scry.CacheOpts.mk .false ".scry-project-cache" .json

/-- Cache policy and statistics. `reason` is `cold`, `hit`, `miss`, `corrupt` or
`disabled` (the first event of the last TU, for the human line). The `lower*` counters
describe the per-TU lowering cache; they are deliberately not printed by
`Scry.CacheStats.line`/`.json`, whose format is a CLI surface (cache statistics are
metadata about the incremental run, not an artifact). -/
structure Scry.CacheStats where
  enabled : Bool
  dir : String
  hit : Nat
  miss : Nat
  write : Nat
  corrupt : Nat
  /-- TUs examined (hit + miss). -/
  files : Nat
  reason : String
  /-- Lowering-cache hits. -/
  lowerHit : Nat
  /-- Lowering-cache misses (`cold`, `miss`, `corrupt`). -/
  lowerMiss : Nat
  /-- Lowering entries written (on a miss). -/
  lowerWrite : Nat
  /-- Lowering entries rejected as corrupt or truncated. -/
  lowerCorrupt : Nat

def Scry.CacheStats.empty : Scry.CacheStats :=
  Scry.CacheStats.mk .false "" 0 0 0 0 0 "disabled" 0 0 0 0

def Scry.CacheStats.enabledWith (dir : String) : Scry.CacheStats :=
  Scry.CacheStats.mk .true dir 0 0 0 0 0 "cold" 0 0 0 0

def Scry.CacheStats.hitOne (s : Scry.CacheStats) : Scry.CacheStats :=
  Scry.CacheStats.mk s.enabled s.dir (Nat.succ s.hit) s.miss s.write s.corrupt
    (Nat.succ s.files) "hit" s.lowerHit s.lowerMiss s.lowerWrite s.lowerCorrupt

def Scry.CacheStats.missOne (s : Scry.CacheStats) (reason : String) : Scry.CacheStats :=
  let corrupt := match String.eq reason "corrupt" with
    | .true => Nat.succ s.corrupt
    | .false => s.corrupt;
  Scry.CacheStats.mk s.enabled s.dir s.hit (Nat.succ s.miss) s.write corrupt
    (Nat.succ s.files) reason s.lowerHit s.lowerMiss s.lowerWrite s.lowerCorrupt

def Scry.CacheStats.writeOne (s : Scry.CacheStats) : Scry.CacheStats :=
  Scry.CacheStats.mk s.enabled s.dir s.hit s.miss (Nat.succ s.write) s.corrupt s.files
    s.reason s.lowerHit s.lowerMiss s.lowerWrite s.lowerCorrupt

/-- A lowering-cache hit. -/
def Scry.CacheStats.lowerHitOne (s : Scry.CacheStats) : Scry.CacheStats :=
  Scry.CacheStats.mk s.enabled s.dir s.hit s.miss s.write s.corrupt s.files s.reason
    (Nat.succ s.lowerHit) s.lowerMiss s.lowerWrite s.lowerCorrupt

/-- A lowering-cache miss; `corrupt` increments the corruption counter too. -/
def Scry.CacheStats.lowerMissOne (s : Scry.CacheStats) (reason : String) : Scry.CacheStats :=
  let corrupt := match String.eq reason "corrupt" with
    | .true => Nat.succ s.lowerCorrupt
    | .false => s.lowerCorrupt;
  Scry.CacheStats.mk s.enabled s.dir s.hit s.miss s.write s.corrupt s.files s.reason
    s.lowerHit (Nat.succ s.lowerMiss) s.lowerWrite corrupt

/-- A lowering entry written after a miss. -/
def Scry.CacheStats.lowerWriteOne (s : Scry.CacheStats) : Scry.CacheStats :=
  Scry.CacheStats.mk s.enabled s.dir s.hit s.miss s.write s.corrupt s.files s.reason
    s.lowerHit s.lowerMiss (Nat.succ s.lowerWrite) s.lowerCorrupt

/-- The cache-entry format version. Bump it when `cacheEntryText`/`cacheEntryOf` change;
an entry written by another version is a clean miss and is rewritten. -/
def Scry.projectCacheMagic : String := "scry-project-tu 3"

/-- The frontend stamp of a cached CST: everything outside the preprocessed text that can
change what `Scry.Native.parse` + `Scry.Native.toCstStore` produce for that text. Bump it
in the same commit as any of:

* the tree-sitter runtime (`scry/native/tree-sitter`, v0.27.2), the C grammar
  (`scry/native/tree-sitter-c`, v0.24.2) or its `LANGUAGE_VERSION` (ABI 15);
* `scry/native/ts_shim.c` (the native store layout, flags or error counting);
* `Scry.Native.parse`/`toCstStore` (kind/field id assignment, span conversion);
* the `scry-cst 1` spill (`Scry.CstStore.render`/`ofText`);
* the preprocessing policy that turns `cc -E` output into the `pre` text
  (`Scry.Preproc.preprocess`, `Scry.markerPaths`, `Scry.normalisePath`,
  `Scry.ProjectIO.stripSystem`, `Scry.Project.isSystemPath`).

A stale CST is worse than a reparse, so this is a deliberate version stamp rather than a
guess about the grammar's own version at run time (see the module doc). -/
def Scry.projectCacheFrontend : String := "cst-1 ts-0.27.2 c-0.24.2 abi-15"

/-- One length-prefixed key field: a field that contains the `\u{0}` separator (or the
digit/colon prefix) cannot make two different inputs share a key. -/
def Scry.cacheKeyField (s : String) : String :=
  String.append (Nat.repr (String.length s)) (String.append ":" s)

/-- A cache key: the format salt, the frontend stamp, the resolved source path, the path
the CST was built with (it is interned in the store's path table), the source text, the
flags and the two options that select the cached result. -/
def Scry.cacheKeyFor (frontend path storedPath text : String) (flags : List String)
    (allowErrors keepSystem : Bool) : String :=
  let mode := match allowErrors with | .true => "allow-errors" | .false => "strict";
  let system := match keepSystem with | .true => "keep-system" | .false => "blank-system";
  let fields := List.append [Scry.projectCacheMagic, frontend, path, storedPath, text]
    (List.append flags [mode, system]);
  let body := String.intercalate "\u{0}" (List.map Scry.cacheKeyField fields);
  Nat.repr (Scry.fnv body)

/-- The cache key of a translation unit under this build's frontend. `path` is the
resolved source path and `storedPath` the spelling `Scry.Native.toCstStore` interns. -/
def Scry.cacheKey (path storedPath text : String) (flags : List String)
    (allowErrors keepSystem : Bool) : String :=
  Scry.cacheKeyFor Scry.projectCacheFrontend path storedPath text flags allowErrors
    keepSystem

/-- The validation digest: the preprocessed text (so header/macro changes invalidate an
entry even when the key is unchanged). -/
def Scry.preDigest (pre : String) : String := Nat.repr (Scry.fnv pre)

-- ## A content digest of every CST column

/-- Mix a string into a running hash (scry's identity hash: multiply then add; the
constants are FNV's, see `Scry.Identity`). -/
def Scry.digestStr (h : U64) (s : String) : U64 :=
  List.foldl (fun acc c => Scry.fnvStep acc (U64.ofNat (Char.code c)))
    (Scry.fnvStep h (U64.ofNat (String.length s))) (String.chars s)

/-- Mix an intern table (its names, in id order) into a running hash. -/
def Scry.digestTable (h : U64) (t : InternTable) : U64 :=
  List.foldl (fun acc s => Scry.digestStr acc s) h (InternTable.toList t)

/-- Mix a `U32` column, with its length, into a running hash. -/
def Scry.digestU32 (h : U64) (a : U32Array) : U64 :=
  U32Array.foldl (fun acc x => Scry.fnvStep acc (U64.ofNat (UIntN.toNat x)))
    (Scry.fnvStep h (U64.ofNat (U32Array.size a))) a

/-- Mix a `U64` column, with its length, into a running hash. -/
def Scry.digestU64 (h : U64) (a : U64Array) : U64 :=
  U64Array.foldl (fun acc x => Scry.fnvStep acc x)
    (Scry.fnvStep h (U64.ofNat (U64Array.size a))) a

/-- Mix a list of naturals (with its length) into a running hash. -/
def Scry.digestNats (h : U64) (xs : List Nat) : U64 :=
  List.foldl (fun acc n => Scry.fnvStep acc (U64.ofNat n))
    (Scry.fnvStep h (U64.ofNat (List.length xs))) xs

/-- The lowering cache's preprocessing digest: the preprocessed text **and the line map**
(`lineStart`/`lineFile`/`lineOrig`), the original paths and texts. `Scry.preDigest` (the
parse cache's) covers only `pre.text`, but a `#line` rewrite of the same length blanks to
the same text; the lowering reads `lineOrig` to stamp every origin and warning, so its key
must cover the map too (H5). -/
def Scry.lowerPreDigest (pre : Scry.Preprocessed) : String :=
  let h0 := Scry.digestStr Scry.fnvOffset pre.text;
  let h1 := List.foldl (fun acc s => Scry.digestStr acc s) h0 pre.paths;
  let h2 := List.foldl (fun acc s => Scry.digestStr acc s) h1 pre.texts;
  let h3 := Scry.digestNats h2 pre.lineStart;
  let h4 := Scry.digestNats h3 pre.lineFile;
  let h5 := Scry.digestNats h4 pre.lineOrig;
  Nat.repr (UIntN.toNat h5)

/-- A digest of **every** column of a CST store, including the intern tables and the
child CSR. `CstStore.render`/`ofText` does not write `nextSib` or the child CSR
(`ofText` re-derives them from the edge rows), so a spill round trip that loses a column
has a different digest even when its rendered text is byte-identical. The cache records
this digest and refuses a loaded entry whose recomputed digest does not match; that turns
any lossy round trip into a cache miss instead of a stale hit.

Collisions are 64-bit; this is a fast consistency check, not a security commitment. -/
def Scry.cstFullDigest (s : Scry.CstStore) : String :=
  let h0 := Scry.digestStr Scry.fnvOffset s.language;
  let h1 := Scry.digestTable h0 s.kinds;
  let h2 := Scry.digestTable h1 s.fields;
  let h3 := Scry.digestTable h2 s.paths;
  let h4 := Scry.digestU32 h3 s.kindCol;
  let h5 := Scry.digestU32 h4 s.fieldCol;
  let h6 := Scry.digestU32 h5 s.flags;
  let h7 := Scry.digestU32 h6 s.parent;
  let h8 := Scry.digestU64 h7 s.spans;
  let h9 := Scry.digestU32 h8 s.fileCol;
  let h10 := Scry.digestU32 h9 s.nextSib;
  let h11 := Scry.digestU32 h10 s.childOff;
  let h12 := Scry.digestU32 h11 s.children;
  let h13 := Scry.digestU32 h12 s.childField;
  let h14 := Scry.digestU32 h13 s.edgeSrc;
  let h15 := Scry.digestU32 h14 s.edgeDst;
  let h16 := Scry.digestU32 h15 s.edgeLabel;
  let h17 := Scry.digestU64 h16 s.bytes;
  let h18 := Scry.digestTable h17 s.relNames;
  let h19 := Scry.digestU32 h18 s.relArity;
  let h20 := Scry.digestU32 h19 s.relOff;
  let h21 := Scry.digestU32 h20 s.relTuples;
  Nat.repr (UIntN.toNat h21)

/-- Create a directory and its missing parents. -/
partial def Scry.mkdirp (dir : String) : EIO Unit :=
  match String.isEmpty dir with
  | .true => EIO.pure ()
  | .false => do
    there ← EIO.lift (FS.exists dir);
    match there with
    | .true => EIO.pure ()
    | .false => do
      let parent := match String.splitOn dir "/" with
        | .nil => ""
        | .cons _ .nil => ""
        | parts => String.intercalate "/" (List.dropLast parts);
      Scry.mkdirp parent;
      FS.createDir dir

/-- The entry path for a key. -/
def Scry.cachePath (dir key : String) : String :=
  String.append dir (String.append "/" (String.append key ".tu"))

/-- The cache entry text. `digest` is `Scry.cstFullDigest` of the store the body
was rendered from: on load the recomputed digest must match, so any lossy round trip (a
column the spill does not write, a different id assignment) is a miss, not a stale hit. -/
def Scry.cacheEntryText (key pre errors digest cst : String) : String :=
  Scry.projectCacheMagic ++ "\nfrontend " ++ Scry.projectCacheFrontend
    ++ "\nkey " ++ key ++ "\npre " ++ pre ++ "\ndigest " ++ digest
    ++ "\nerrors " ++ errors
    ++ "\nbytes " ++ Nat.repr (String.length cst) ++ "\n" ++ cst

/-- Parse a cache entry. Returns `(store, errors)` or a reason: `miss` for an entry
written by another format or frontend (it is recomputed and rewritten, not corruption),
`corrupt` for a malformed or truncated body, or a body whose recomputed full-column
digest is not the recorded one. -/
def Scry.cacheEntryOf (key pre text : String) :
    Except String (Scry.CstStore × Nat) :=
  match String.splitOn text "\n" with
  | .cons magic rest =>
    match String.eq magic Scry.projectCacheMagic with
    | .false => match String.startsWith magic "scry-project-tu " with
      | .true => .error "miss"
      | .false => .error "corrupt"
    | .true => match rest with
      | .cons frontLine (.cons keyLine (.cons preLine (.cons digestLine
          (.cons errLine (.cons bytesLine rest2))))) =>
        match String.startsWith frontLine "frontend " with
        | .false => .error "corrupt"
        | .true => match String.eq (String.drop 9 frontLine) Scry.projectCacheFrontend with
          | .false => .error "miss"
          | .true => match String.startsWith keyLine "key " with
            | .false => .error "corrupt"
            | .true => match String.startsWith preLine "pre " with
              | .false => .error "corrupt"
              | .true => match String.startsWith digestLine "digest " with
                | .false => .error "corrupt"
                | .true => match String.startsWith errLine "errors " with
                  | .false => .error "corrupt"
                  | .true => match String.startsWith bytesLine "bytes " with
                    | .false => .error "corrupt"
                    | .true =>
                      let k := String.drop 4 keyLine;
                      let p := String.drop 4 preLine;
                      let dg := String.drop 7 digestLine;
                      let es := String.drop 7 errLine;
                      let bs := String.drop 6 bytesLine;
                      let cst := String.intercalate "\n" rest2;
                      match Bool.and (String.eq k key) (String.eq p pre) with
                      | .false => .error "miss"
                      | .true => match String.parseNat bs with
                        | .none => .error "corrupt"
                        | .some n => match Nat.beq n (String.length cst) with
                          | .false => .error "corrupt"
                          | .true => match String.parseNat es with
                            | .none => .error "corrupt"
                            | .some e => match Scry.CstStore.ofText cst with
                              | .error _ => .error "corrupt"
                              | .ok st => match String.eq (Scry.cstFullDigest st) dg with
                                | .true => .ok (st, e)
                                | .false => .error "corrupt"
      | _ => .error "corrupt"
  | .nil => .error "corrupt"

/-- Read a cache entry; an absent entry is `cold`, an unreadable one `corrupt` (the run
recomputes instead of failing), and a mismatched one `miss`. -/
def Scry.cacheLoad (dir key pre : String) : EIO (Except String (Scry.CstStore × Nat)) := do
  let p := Scry.cachePath dir key;
  there ← EIO.lift (FS.exists p);
  match there with
  | .false => EIO.pure (.error "cold")
  | .true => do
    textRes ← EIO.lift (FS.readFile p);
    match textRes with
    | .error _ => EIO.pure (.error "corrupt")
    | .ok text => EIO.pure (Scry.cacheEntryOf key pre text)

/-- Write `text` to `p` atomically: a sibling `.tmp`, then rename over the target. A
`.tmp` that is a symbolic link is refused: `FS.writeFile` opens with `O_TRUNC`, so a
committed `<entry>.tmp -> ~/.bashrc` would otherwise truncate the target. Both cache
formats write exactly one record through this function. -/
def Scry.cacheWriteAtomic (p text : String) : EIO Unit := do
  let tmp := String.append p ".tmp";
  st ← EIO.lift (FS.linkStat tmp);
  match st with
  | .ok s => match s.kind with
    | .symlink => EIO.fail ("cache: " ++ tmp ++ " is a symbolic link; refusing to write through it")
    | _ => EIO.pure ()
  | .error _ => EIO.pure ();
  FS.writeFile tmp text;
  FS.rename tmp p

/-- Write a cache entry atomically (sibling `.tmp`, then rename over the target). -/
def Scry.cacheSave (dir key pre errors digest cst : String) : EIO Unit := do
  Scry.mkdirp dir;
  Scry.cacheWriteAtomic (Scry.cachePath dir key)
    (Scry.cacheEntryText key pre errors digest cst)

/-- Overwrite every entry in `dir` with a malformed body (the corruption test). -/
partial def Scry.corruptCacheEntries (dir : String) : List String → EIO Unit
  | .nil => EIO.pure ()
  | .cons name rest => do
    FS.writeFile (String.append dir (String.append "/" name)) "not a cache entry\n";
    Scry.corruptCacheEntries dir rest

-- ## The canonical Wil dump (shared with `scry-artifacts`)

/-- A line for the canonical dump. -/
def Scry.adLine (xs : List String) : String := String.intercalate "\t" xs

/-- Lines joined with a final newline. -/
def Scry.adLines (ls : List String) : String :=
  List.foldr (fun l acc => l ++ "\n" ++ acc) "" ls

/-- `f 0, f 1, …, f (n-1)`, by index (structural on the fuel). -/
partial def Scry.adRangeGo (f : Nat → String) : Nat → Nat → List String
  | .zero, _ => .nil
  | .succ k, i => List.cons (f i) (Scry.adRangeGo f k (Nat.succ i))

def Scry.adRange (n : Nat) (f : Nat → String) : List String := Scry.adRangeGo f n 0

/-- A type. -/
def Scry.adTy : Wil.Ty → String
  | .int w s => "int" ++ Nat.repr w ++ (match s with | .true => "s" | .false => "u")
  | .ptr t => "ptr(" ++ Scry.adTy t ++ ")"
  | .fn n t => "fn(" ++ Nat.repr n ++ "," ++ Scry.adTy t ++ ")"
  | .record i => "record" ++ Nat.repr i
  | .dyn => "dyn"
  | .unknown => "unknown"

/-- A concrete value. -/
def Scry.adVal : Wil.Val → String
  | .int w s b => "int" ++ Nat.repr w ++ (match s with | .true => "s" | .false => "u")
      ++ ":" ++ Nat.repr b
  | .ptr b o => "ptr:" ++ Nat.repr b ++ ":" ++ Nat.repr o
  | .nul => "null"
  | .undef => "undef"

/-- A register: index and declared type. -/
def Scry.adVar (v : Wil.Var) : String :=
  Nat.repr v.index ++ ":" ++ Scry.adTy v.ty

/-- An operator. -/
def Scry.adOp : Wil.Op → String
  | .add => "add" | .sub => "sub" | .mul => "mul" | .div => "div" | .rem => "rem"
  | .and => "and" | .or => "or" | .xor => "xor" | .shl => "shl" | .shr => "shr"
  | .eq => "eq" | .ne => "ne" | .lt => "lt" | .le => "le" | .gt => "gt" | .ge => "ge"
  | .neg => "neg" | .not => "not"

/-- A condition. -/
def Scry.adCond : Wil.Cond → String
  | .const b => "const" ++ (match b with | .true => "1" | .false => "0")
  | .cmp op a b => "cmp:" ++ Scry.adOp op ++ ":" ++ Scry.adVar a ++ ":" ++ Scry.adVar b
  | .not c => "not(" ++ Scry.adCond c ++ ")"
  | .and a b => "and(" ++ Scry.adCond a ++ "," ++ Scry.adCond b ++ ")"
  | .or a b => "or(" ++ Scry.adCond a ++ "," ++ Scry.adCond b ++ ")"

/-- A right-hand side. -/
def Scry.adRhs : Wil.Rhs → String
  | .use v => "use:" ++ Scry.adVar v
  | .cst v => "cst:" ++ Scry.adVal v
  | .bin op a b => "bin:" ++ Scry.adOp op ++ ":" ++ Scry.adVar a ++ ":" ++ Scry.adVar b
  | .un op a => "un:" ++ Scry.adOp op ++ ":" ++ Scry.adVar a
  | .load p => "load:" ++ Scry.adVar p
  | .gep p k => "gep:" ++ Scry.adVar p ++ ":" ++ Nat.repr k
  | .alloc n => "alloc:" ++ Nat.repr n
  | .havoc t => "havoc:" ++ Scry.adTy t
  | .slot n => "slot:" ++ Nat.repr n
  | .globalRef n => "global:" ++ n
  | .fnptr n => "fnptr:" ++ n
  | .gepVar p i k => "gepvar:" ++ Scry.adVar p ++ ":" ++ Scry.adVar i ++ ":" ++ Nat.repr k

/-- A callee. -/
def Scry.adCallee : Wil.Callee → String
  | .direct n => "direct:" ++ n
  | .indirect v => "indirect:" ++ Scry.adVar v

/-- An origin: file, byte range, CST node, line, column. -/
def Scry.adSpan (sp : Wil.Span) : String :=
  Scry.adLine [Nat.repr sp.file, Nat.repr sp.start, Nat.repr sp.stop, Nat.repr sp.node,
    Nat.repr sp.line, Nat.repr sp.col]

/-- A statement (one line). -/
def Scry.adStmt : Wil.Stmt → String
  | .assign x e o => "assign\t" ++ Scry.adVar x ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adRhs e ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adSpan o
  | .assume c o => "assume\t" ++ Scry.adCond c ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adSpan o
  | .store p v o => "store\t" ++ Scry.adVar p ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adVar v ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adSpan o
  | .free p o => "free\t" ++ Scry.adVar p ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adSpan o
  | .call r c args o => "call\t"
      ++ (match r with | .some v => Scry.adVar v | .none => "-")
      ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adCallee c
      ++ "\t" ++ String.intercalate "," (List.map Scry.adVar args)
      ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adSpan o
  | .nop o => "nop\t" ++ Scry.adSpan o

/-- A terminator (one line). -/
def Scry.adTerm : Wil.Term → String
  | .goto b o => "goto\t" ++ Nat.repr b.index ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adSpan o
  | .br c t f o => "br\t" ++ Scry.adCond c ++ "\t" ++ Nat.repr t.index ++ "\t"
      ++ Nat.repr f.index ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adSpan o
  | .ret v o => "ret\t" ++ (match v with | .some x => Scry.adVar x | .none => "-")
      ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adSpan o
  | .unreachable o => "unreachable\t" ++ Scry.adSpan o

/-- One block's statements and terminator, each prefixed with its block and statement
index. -/
partial def Scry.adStmtsGo (b i : Nat) : List Wil.Stmt → List String
  | .nil => .nil
  | .cons s rest => List.cons
      ("b" ++ Nat.repr b ++ "\ts" ++ Nat.repr i ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adStmt s)
      (Scry.adStmtsGo b (Nat.succ i) rest)

/-- Every block of a function, in order. -/
partial def Scry.adBlocksGo (i : Nat) : List Wil.Block → List String
  | .nil => .nil
  | .cons blk rest => List.append
      (Scry.adStmtsGo i 0 blk.stmts)
      (List.cons ("b" ++ Nat.repr i ++ "\tterm\t" ++ Scry.adTerm blk.term)
        (Scry.adBlocksGo (Nat.succ i) rest))

/-- One function: name, entry, params, locals, origin, blocks. -/
def Scry.adFunc (i : Nat) (f : Wil.Func) : List String :=
  List.append
    [ "func\t" ++ Nat.repr i ++ "\t" ++ f.name
    , "entry\t" ++ Nat.repr f.entry.index
    , "origin\t" ++ Scry.adSpan f.origin
    , "params\t" ++ String.intercalate "," (List.map Scry.adVar f.params)
    , "locals\t" ++ String.intercalate "," (List.map Scry.adVar f.locals) ]
    (Scry.adBlocksGo 0 f.blocks)

/-- Every function of a TU, with its index. -/
partial def Scry.adFuncsGo (i : Nat) : List Wil.Func → List String
  | .nil => .nil
  | .cons f rest => List.append (Scry.adFunc i f) (Scry.adFuncsGo (Nat.succ i) rest)

/-- One warning: function, message, origin. -/
def Scry.adWarning (w : Scry.Warning) : String :=
  "warning\t" ++ w.func ++ "\t" ++ w.message ++ "\t" ++ Scry.adSpan w.span

/-- Every warning, in the TU's order (newest first). -/
def Scry.adWarnings (ws : List Scry.Warning) : List String :=
  List.map (fun w => Scry.adWarning w) ws

/-- Every external call, in the TU's order. -/
def Scry.adExternals (xs : List (String × String)) : List String :=
  List.map (fun p => "external\t" ++ p.1 ++ "\t" ++ p.2) xs

/-- The source files of a TU: index, path, text length, and a 64-bit digest of the
text (so a line-map/text change is visible even when the CST is identical). -/
def Scry.adFiles (tu : Scry.TU) : List String :=
  List.append [ "files\t" ++ Nat.repr (List.length tu.files) ]
    (List.map (fun p =>
      let i := p.1;
      let text := p.2;
      "file\t" ++ Nat.repr i ++ "\t" ++ Scry.cstEnc (List.getD tu.paths i "")
        ++ "\t" ++ Nat.repr (String.length text) ++ "\t" ++ Nat.repr (Scry.fnv text))
      (Scry.zipIdx tu.files 0))

/-- The canonical dump of one lowered translation unit: path, error count, CST digest,
files, every function/block/statement/terminator with its origin, warnings and external
calls. This is the `.tu` shape `scry-artifacts` writes and the differential harness
compares; the lowering cache stores the same content (in a versioned JSON record). -/
def Scry.tuDump (tu : Scry.TU) (path : String) (errors : Nat)
    (storeDigest : String) : String :=
  let files := Scry.adFiles tu;
  let funcs := Scry.adFuncsGo 0 tu.funcs;
  let warnings := Scry.adWarnings tu.warnings;
  let externals := Scry.adExternals tu.externalCalls;
  Scry.adLines (List.concat
    [ [ "unit\t" ++ Scry.cstEnc path
      , "errors\t" ++ Nat.repr errors
      , "store-digest\t" ++ storeDigest ]
    , files
    , [ "funcs\t" ++ Nat.repr (List.length tu.funcs) ]
    , funcs
    , [ "warnings\t" ++ Nat.repr (List.length tu.warnings) ]
    , warnings
    , [ "externals\t" ++ Nat.repr (List.length tu.externalCalls) ]
    , externals ])

-- ## The lowering cache

/-- The lowering-entry format. Bump it when the JSON writer/reader below changes; an
entry from another format is a clean miss and is rewritten. -/
def Scry.lowerCacheMagic : String := "scry-project-lower 1"

/-- The identity of the lowering that produced a cached `Scry.TU`. It is the manual
contract (like `Scry.projectCacheFrontend`): bump it in the same commit as any change to
`Scry.lowerTU`/`Scry.lowerTUWithModels`, `Scry.lowerFuncs`, the state-machine passes, or
`Scry.defaultModels`. A stale `Scry.TU` is worse than a re-lowering, so this is a
deliberate version stamp rather than a guess. -/
def Scry.projectLowererVersion : String := "c-lower-1 default-models"

/-- The lowering-cache entry path: a distinct prefix in the same cache directory as the
parse entries (whose names are bare decimal keys), so one `--cache-dir` holds both. -/
def Scry.lowerCachePath (dir key : String) : String :=
  String.append dir (String.append "/lower-" (String.append key ".tu"))

/-- The per-file key fields: the path and a digest of the original text, in file order.
Origins and warnings carry original positions, which come from the line map and texts,
not from the preprocessed text (H5 in `wiz-notes/incremental-scry.md`). -/
partial def Scry.lowerKeyFiles : List String → List String → List String
  | .nil, _ => .nil
  | .cons p ps, texts => match texts with
    | .nil => List.cons (Scry.cacheKeyField p) (Scry.lowerKeyFiles ps .nil)
    | .cons t ts =>
      List.cons (Scry.cacheKeyField p)
        (List.cons (Scry.cacheKeyField (Nat.repr (String.length t)))
          (List.cons (Scry.cacheKeyField (Nat.repr (Scry.fnv t)))
            (Scry.lowerKeyFiles ps ts)))

/-- The lowering key: the full set of inputs the lowering reads — the entry format, the
lowerer identity, the full CST digest, the preprocessing digest (the preprocessed text
**and its line map**), the unit's target path, the original file paths and text digests,
and the recovery mode. It is deliberately **not** the parse cache's outer key: the CST
key also covers the raw source text and preprocessing flags, which lowering never reads
directly, while the lowering key needs the per-file text digests and line map the CST key
does not have. -/
def Scry.lowerCacheKeyFor (lowerer cst pre target : String) (paths texts : List String)
    (allowErrors : Bool) : String :=
  let mode := match allowErrors with | .true => "allow-errors" | .false => "strict";
  let fields := List.append
    [Scry.lowerCacheMagic, lowerer, cst, pre, target, mode,
     Nat.repr (List.length paths), Nat.repr (List.length texts)]
    (Scry.lowerKeyFiles paths texts);
  let body := String.intercalate "\u{0}" (List.map Scry.cacheKeyField fields);
  Nat.repr (Scry.fnv body)

/-- The lowering key under this build's lowerer identity. -/
def Scry.lowerCacheKey (cst pre target : String) (paths texts : List String)
    (allowErrors : Bool) : String :=
  Scry.lowerCacheKeyFor Scry.projectLowererVersion cst pre target paths texts allowErrors

-- ### The versioned JSON record

/-- The first element of a tagged JSON array and its remaining elements. -/
def Scry.lcTagArgs (j : Json) : Option (String × List Json) := do
  let xs := Json.asList j;
  t ← Json.asString (List.getD xs 0 Json.null);
  .some (t, List.drop 1 xs)

/-- A required string field of a JSON object. -/
def Scry.lcStrField (j : Json) (k : String) : Option String :=
  match Json.get j k with
  | .some v => Json.asString v
  | .none => .none

/-- A required natural field of a JSON object. -/
def Scry.lcNatField (j : Json) (k : String) : Option Nat :=
  match Json.get j k with
  | .some v => Json.asNat v
  | .none => .none

/-- A required array field of a JSON object. -/
def Scry.lcArrField (j : Json) (k : String) : Option (List Json) :=
  match Json.get j k with
  | .some v => .some (Json.asList v)
  | .none => .none

/-- `f` over every element, or `none` if any element does not decode (so a malformed
element is corruption, not a silently shorter list). -/
partial def Scry.lcAllOpt {A : Type} (f : Json → Option A) : List Json → Option (List A)
  | .nil => .some .nil
  | .cons x xs => do
    y ← f x;
    ys ← Scry.lcAllOpt f xs;
    .some (List.cons y ys)

/-- A JSON array of naturals, decoded strictly. -/
partial def Scry.lcNatListOf : List Json → Option (List Nat)
  | .nil => .some .nil
  | .cons x xs => do
    n ← Json.asNat x;
    ns ← Scry.lcNatListOf xs;
    .some (List.cons n ns)

/-- A `Wil.Ty` as JSON: `["int", w, signed]`, `["ptr", t]`, `["fn", n, t]`,
`["record", id]`, `"dyn"` or `"unknown"`. -/
def Scry.lcTyJson : Wil.Ty → Json
  | .int w s => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "int", Json.ofNat w, Json.mkBool s]
  | .ptr t => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "ptr", Scry.lcTyJson t]
  | .fn n t => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "fn", Json.ofNat n, Scry.lcTyJson t]
  | .record i => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "record", Json.ofNat i]
  | .dyn => Json.mkStr "dyn"
  | .unknown => Json.mkStr "unknown"

partial def Scry.lcTyOfJson (j : Json) : Option Wil.Ty :=
  match Json.asString j with
  | .some s => match s with
    | "dyn" => .some .dyn
    | "unknown" => .some .unknown
    | _ => .none
  | .none => do
    pair ← Scry.lcTagArgs j;
    match pair.1, pair.2 with
    | "int", .cons w (.cons s .nil) => do
      wn ← Json.asNat w;
      sn ← Json.asBool s;
      .some (.int wn sn)
    | "ptr", .cons t .nil => do
      tt ← Scry.lcTyOfJson t;
      .some (.ptr tt)
    | "fn", .cons n (.cons t .nil) => do
      nn ← Json.asNat n;
      tt ← Scry.lcTyOfJson t;
      .some (.fn nn tt)
    | "record", .cons i .nil => do
      ii ← Json.asNat i;
      .some (.record ii)
    | _, _ => .none

/-- A `Wil.Val` as JSON. -/
def Scry.lcValJson : Wil.Val → Json
  | .int w s b => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "int", Json.ofNat w, Json.mkBool s, Json.ofNat b]
  | .ptr b o => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "ptr", Json.ofNat b, Json.ofNat o]
  | .nul => Json.mkStr "nul"
  | .undef => Json.mkStr "undef"

def Scry.lcValOfJson (j : Json) : Option Wil.Val :=
  match Json.asString j with
  | .some s => match s with
    | "nul" => .some .nul
    | "undef" => .some .undef
    | _ => .none
  | .none => do
    pair ← Scry.lcTagArgs j;
    match pair.1, pair.2 with
    | "int", .cons w (.cons s (.cons b .nil)) => do
      wn ← Json.asNat w;
      sn ← Json.asBool s;
      bn ← Json.asNat b;
      .some (.int wn sn bn)
    | "ptr", .cons b (.cons o .nil) => do
      bn ← Json.asNat b;
      on ← Json.asNat o;
      .some (.ptr bn on)
    | _, _ => .none

/-- A `Wil.Var` as `[index, ty]`. -/
def Scry.lcVarJson (v : Wil.Var) : Json :=
  Json.mkArr [Json.ofNat v.index, Scry.lcTyJson v.ty]

def Scry.lcVarOfJson (j : Json) : Option Wil.Var := do
  let xs := Json.asList j;
  i ← Json.asNat (List.getD xs 0 Json.null);
  t ← Scry.lcTyOfJson (List.getD xs 1 Json.null);
  .some (Wil.Var.mk i t)

/-- A `Wil.Span` as `[file, start, stop, node, line, col]`. -/
def Scry.lcSpanJson (sp : Wil.Span) : Json :=
  Json.mkArr [Json.ofNat sp.file, Json.ofNat sp.start, Json.ofNat sp.stop,
    Json.ofNat sp.node, Json.ofNat sp.line, Json.ofNat sp.col]

def Scry.lcSpanOfJson (j : Json) : Option Wil.Span :=
  match Scry.lcNatListOf (Json.asList j) with
  | .some (.cons file (.cons start (.cons stop (.cons node (.cons line (.cons col .nil)))))) =>
    .some (Wil.Span.mk file start stop node line col)
  | _ => .none

/-- An optional variable as `["none"]` or `["some", var]`. -/
def Scry.lcOptVarJson : Option Wil.Var → Json
  | .none => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "none"]
  | .some v => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "some", Scry.lcVarJson v]

def Scry.lcOptVarOfJson (j : Json) : Option (Option Wil.Var) := do
  pair ← Scry.lcTagArgs j;
  match pair.1, pair.2 with
  | "none", .nil => .some (.none : Option Wil.Var)
  | "some", .cons v .nil => do
    vv ← Scry.lcVarOfJson v;
    .some (.some vv)
  | _, _ => .none

/-- A `Wil.Cond`. -/
def Scry.lcCondJson : Wil.Cond → Json
  | .const b => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "const", Json.mkBool b]
  | .cmp op a b => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "cmp", Json.mkStr (Scry.adOp op),
      Scry.lcVarJson a, Scry.lcVarJson b]
  | .not c => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "not", Scry.lcCondJson c]
  | .and a b => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "and", Scry.lcCondJson a, Scry.lcCondJson b]
  | .or a b => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "or", Scry.lcCondJson a, Scry.lcCondJson b]

def Scry.lcOpOfName (s : String) : Option Wil.Op :=
  match s with
  | "add" => .some .add
  | "sub" => .some .sub
  | "mul" => .some .mul
  | "div" => .some .div
  | "rem" => .some .rem
  | "and" => .some .and
  | "or" => .some .or
  | "xor" => .some .xor
  | "shl" => .some .shl
  | "shr" => .some .shr
  | "eq" => .some .eq
  | "ne" => .some .ne
  | "lt" => .some .lt
  | "le" => .some .le
  | "gt" => .some .gt
  | "ge" => .some .ge
  | "neg" => .some .neg
  | "not" => .some .not
  | _ => .none

partial def Scry.lcCondOfJson (j : Json) : Option Wil.Cond := do
  pair ← Scry.lcTagArgs j;
  match pair.1, pair.2 with
  | "const", .cons b .nil => do
    bb ← Json.asBool b;
    .some (.const bb)
  | "cmp", .cons op (.cons a (.cons b .nil)) => do
    on ← Json.asString op;
    oo ← Scry.lcOpOfName on;
    va ← Scry.lcVarOfJson a;
    vb ← Scry.lcVarOfJson b;
    .some (.cmp oo va vb)
  | "not", .cons c .nil => do
    cc ← Scry.lcCondOfJson c;
    .some (.not cc)
  | "and", .cons a (.cons b .nil) => do
    ca ← Scry.lcCondOfJson a;
    cb ← Scry.lcCondOfJson b;
    .some (.and ca cb)
  | "or", .cons a (.cons b .nil) => do
    ca ← Scry.lcCondOfJson a;
    cb ← Scry.lcCondOfJson b;
    .some (.or ca cb)
  | _, _ => .none

/-- A `Wil.Rhs`. -/
def Scry.lcRhsJson : Wil.Rhs → Json
  | .use v => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "use", Scry.lcVarJson v]
  | .cst v => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "cst", Scry.lcValJson v]
  | .bin op a b => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "bin", Json.mkStr (Scry.adOp op),
      Scry.lcVarJson a, Scry.lcVarJson b]
  | .un op a => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "un", Json.mkStr (Scry.adOp op), Scry.lcVarJson a]
  | .load p => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "load", Scry.lcVarJson p]
  | .gep p k => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "gep", Scry.lcVarJson p, Json.ofNat k]
  | .alloc n => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "alloc", Json.ofNat n]
  | .havoc t => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "havoc", Scry.lcTyJson t]
  | .slot n => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "slot", Json.ofNat n]
  | .globalRef n => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "globalRef", Json.mkStr n]
  | .fnptr n => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "fnptr", Json.mkStr n]
  | .gepVar p i k => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "gepVar", Scry.lcVarJson p,
      Scry.lcVarJson i, Json.ofNat k]

def Scry.lcRhsOfJson (j : Json) : Option Wil.Rhs := do
  pair ← Scry.lcTagArgs j;
  match pair.1, pair.2 with
  | "use", .cons v .nil => do
    vv ← Scry.lcVarOfJson v;
    .some (.use vv)
  | "cst", .cons v .nil => do
    vv ← Scry.lcValOfJson v;
    .some (.cst vv)
  | "bin", .cons op (.cons a (.cons b .nil)) => do
    on ← Json.asString op;
    oo ← Scry.lcOpOfName on;
    va ← Scry.lcVarOfJson a;
    vb ← Scry.lcVarOfJson b;
    .some (.bin oo va vb)
  | "un", .cons op (.cons a .nil) => do
    on ← Json.asString op;
    oo ← Scry.lcOpOfName on;
    va ← Scry.lcVarOfJson a;
    .some (.un oo va)
  | "load", .cons p .nil => do
    vp ← Scry.lcVarOfJson p;
    .some (.load vp)
  | "gep", .cons p (.cons k .nil) => do
    vp ← Scry.lcVarOfJson p;
    kk ← Json.asNat k;
    .some (.gep vp kk)
  | "alloc", .cons n .nil => do
    nn ← Json.asNat n;
    .some (.alloc nn)
  | "havoc", .cons t .nil => do
    tt ← Scry.lcTyOfJson t;
    .some (.havoc tt)
  | "slot", .cons n .nil => do
    nn ← Json.asNat n;
    .some (.slot nn)
  | "globalRef", .cons n .nil => do
    nn ← Json.asString n;
    .some (.globalRef nn)
  | "fnptr", .cons n .nil => do
    nn ← Json.asString n;
    .some (.fnptr nn)
  | "gepVar", .cons p (.cons i (.cons k .nil)) => do
    vp ← Scry.lcVarOfJson p;
    vi ← Scry.lcVarOfJson i;
    kk ← Json.asNat k;
    .some (.gepVar vp vi kk)
  | _, _ => .none

/-- A `Wil.Callee`. -/
def Scry.lcCalleeJson : Wil.Callee → Json
  | .direct n => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "direct", Json.mkStr n]
  | .indirect v => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "indirect", Scry.lcVarJson v]

def Scry.lcCalleeOfJson (j : Json) : Option Wil.Callee := do
  pair ← Scry.lcTagArgs j;
  match pair.1, pair.2 with
  | "direct", .cons n .nil => do
    nn ← Json.asString n;
    .some (.direct nn)
  | "indirect", .cons v .nil => do
    vv ← Scry.lcVarOfJson v;
    .some (.indirect vv)
  | _, _ => .none

/-- A `Wil.Stmt`. -/
def Scry.lcStmtJson : Wil.Stmt → Json
  | .assign x e o => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "assign", Scry.lcVarJson x,
      Scry.lcRhsJson e, Scry.lcSpanJson o]
  | .assume c o => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "assume", Scry.lcCondJson c, Scry.lcSpanJson o]
  | .store p v o => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "store", Scry.lcVarJson p, Scry.lcVarJson v,
      Scry.lcSpanJson o]
  | .free p o => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "free", Scry.lcVarJson p, Scry.lcSpanJson o]
  | .call r c args o => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "call", Scry.lcOptVarJson r,
      Scry.lcCalleeJson c, Json.mkArr (List.map Scry.lcVarJson args), Scry.lcSpanJson o]
  | .nop o => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "nop", Scry.lcSpanJson o]

def Scry.lcStmtOfJson (j : Json) : Option Wil.Stmt := do
  pair ← Scry.lcTagArgs j;
  match pair.1, pair.2 with
  | "assign", .cons x (.cons e (.cons o .nil)) => do
    vx ← Scry.lcVarOfJson x;
    ve ← Scry.lcRhsOfJson e;
    vo ← Scry.lcSpanOfJson o;
    .some (.assign vx ve vo)
  | "assume", .cons c (.cons o .nil) => do
    vc ← Scry.lcCondOfJson c;
    vo ← Scry.lcSpanOfJson o;
    .some (.assume vc vo)
  | "store", .cons p (.cons v (.cons o .nil)) => do
    vp ← Scry.lcVarOfJson p;
    vv ← Scry.lcVarOfJson v;
    vo ← Scry.lcSpanOfJson o;
    .some (.store vp vv vo)
  | "free", .cons p (.cons o .nil) => do
    vp ← Scry.lcVarOfJson p;
    vo ← Scry.lcSpanOfJson o;
    .some (.free vp vo)
  | "call", .cons r (.cons c (.cons args (.cons o .nil))) => do
    vr ← Scry.lcOptVarOfJson r;
    vc ← Scry.lcCalleeOfJson c;
    vs ← Scry.lcAllOpt Scry.lcVarOfJson (Json.asList args);
    vo ← Scry.lcSpanOfJson o;
    .some (.call vr vc vs vo)
  | "nop", .cons o .nil => do
    vo ← Scry.lcSpanOfJson o;
    .some (.nop vo)
  | _, _ => .none

/-- A `Wil.Term`. -/
def Scry.lcTermJson : Wil.Term → Json
  | .goto b o => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "goto", Json.ofNat b.index, Scry.lcSpanJson o]
  | .br c t f o => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "br", Scry.lcCondJson c, Json.ofNat t.index,
      Json.ofNat f.index, Scry.lcSpanJson o]
  | .ret v o => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "ret", Scry.lcOptVarJson v, Scry.lcSpanJson o]
  | .unreachable o => Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr "unreachable", Scry.lcSpanJson o]

def Scry.lcTermOfJson (j : Json) : Option Wil.Term := do
  pair ← Scry.lcTagArgs j;
  match pair.1, pair.2 with
  | "goto", .cons b (.cons o .nil) => do
    bb ← Json.asNat b;
    vo ← Scry.lcSpanOfJson o;
    .some (.goto (Wil.mkBlockId bb) vo)
  | "br", .cons c (.cons t (.cons f (.cons o .nil))) => do
    vc ← Scry.lcCondOfJson c;
    bt ← Json.asNat t;
    bf ← Json.asNat f;
    vo ← Scry.lcSpanOfJson o;
    .some (.br vc (Wil.mkBlockId bt) (Wil.mkBlockId bf) vo)
  | "ret", .cons v (.cons o .nil) => do
    vv ← Scry.lcOptVarOfJson v;
    vo ← Scry.lcSpanOfJson o;
    .some (.ret vv vo)
  | "unreachable", .cons o .nil => do
    vo ← Scry.lcSpanOfJson o;
    .some (.unreachable vo)
  | _, _ => .none

/-- A `Wil.Block`. -/
def Scry.lcBlockJson (b : Wil.Block) : Json :=
  Json.mkObj
    [ ("stmts", Json.mkArr (List.map Scry.lcStmtJson b.stmts))
    , ("term", Scry.lcTermJson b.term) ]

def Scry.lcBlockOfJson (j : Json) : Option Wil.Block := do
  ss ← Scry.lcArrField j "stmts";
  stmts ← Scry.lcAllOpt Scry.lcStmtOfJson ss;
  let topt := Json.get j "term";
  t ← match topt with
    | .some x => Scry.lcTermOfJson x
    | .none => .none;
  .some (Wil.Block.mk stmts t)

/-- A `Wil.Func`. -/
def Scry.lcFuncJson (f : Wil.Func) : Json :=
  Json.mkObj
    [ ("name", Json.mkStr f.name)
    , ("entry", Json.ofNat f.entry.index)
    , ("params", Json.mkArr (List.map Scry.lcVarJson f.params))
    , ("locals", Json.mkArr (List.map Scry.lcVarJson f.locals))
    , ("origin", Scry.lcSpanJson f.origin)
    , ("blocks", Json.mkArr (List.map Scry.lcBlockJson f.blocks)) ]

def Scry.lcFuncOfJson (j : Json) : Option Wil.Func := do
  name ← Scry.lcStrField j "name";
  entry ← Scry.lcNatField j "entry";
  ps ← Scry.lcArrField j "params";
  params ← Scry.lcAllOpt Scry.lcVarOfJson ps;
  ls ← Scry.lcArrField j "locals";
  locals ← Scry.lcAllOpt Scry.lcVarOfJson ls;
  let oopt := Json.get j "origin";
  o ← match oopt with
    | .some x => Scry.lcSpanOfJson x
    | .none => .none;
  bs ← Scry.lcArrField j "blocks";
  blocks ← Scry.lcAllOpt Scry.lcBlockOfJson bs;
  .some (Wil.Func.mk name params locals (Wil.mkBlockId entry) blocks o)

/-- A `Scry.Warning`. -/
def Scry.lcWarningJson (w : Scry.Warning) : Json :=
  Json.mkObj
    [ ("func", Json.mkStr w.func)
    , ("message", Json.mkStr w.message)
    , ("span", Scry.lcSpanJson w.span) ]

def Scry.lcWarningOfJson (j : Json) : Option Scry.Warning := do
  func ← Scry.lcStrField j "func";
  message ← Scry.lcStrField j "message";
  let sopt := Json.get j "span";
  sp ← match sopt with
    | .some x => Scry.lcSpanOfJson x
    | .none => .none;
  .some (Scry.Warning.mk func message sp)

/-- An external call as `[caller, callee]`. -/
def Scry.lcExternalJson (p : String × String) : Json :=
  Json.mkArr [Json.mkStr p.1, Json.mkStr p.2]

def Scry.lcExternalOfJson (j : Json) : Option (String × String) :=
  match Json.asList j with
  | .cons a (.cons b .nil) => match Json.asString a, Json.asString b with
    | .some x, .some y => .some (x, y)
    | _, _ => .none
  | _ => .none

/-- The lowering entry's body: one JSON line per function, warning and external call,
in the TU's canonical order. `Scry.adLines` gives every line (including the last) a
trailing newline, so the body is self-delimiting. -/
def Scry.lowerBodyOf (tu : Scry.TU) : String :=
  Scry.adLines (List.append
    (List.map (fun f => "func\t" ++ Json.render (Scry.lcFuncJson f)) tu.funcs)
    (List.append
      (List.map (fun w => "warning\t" ++ Json.render (Scry.lcWarningJson w)) tu.warnings)
      (List.map (fun e => "external\t" ++ Json.render (Scry.lcExternalJson e))
        tu.externalCalls)))

/-- The TU's file texts, padded with `""` to the path count. `Scry.lowerTUWithModels` reads
`fileText i` for `i < List.length pre.paths` and `getD` returns `""` past the text list;
the cache must reconstruct exactly that list. -/
partial def Scry.lowerPadTexts : List String → List String → List String
  | .nil, _ => .nil
  | .cons p ps, texts => match texts with
    | .cons t ts => List.cons t (Scry.lowerPadTexts ps ts)
    | .nil => List.cons "" (Scry.lowerPadTexts ps .nil)

/-- The three lists a lowering body reconstructs. A structure (not a triple): the
elaborator does not flatten pattern matches on nested pairs. -/
structure Scry.LowerBody where
  funcs : List Wil.Func
  warnings : List Scry.Warning
  externals : List (String × String)

/-- Parse the body lines. Every line must be a `func`, `warning` or `external` record and
decode; anything else is corruption (never a partial TU). -/
partial def Scry.lcBodyParseGo (funcs : List Wil.Func) (warnings : List Scry.Warning)
    (externals : List (String × String)) : List String →
    Except String Scry.LowerBody
  | .nil => .ok (Scry.LowerBody.mk (List.reverse funcs) (List.reverse warnings)
      (List.reverse externals))
  | .cons line rest =>
    match String.stripPrefix line "func\t" with
    | .some js => match Json.parse js with
      | .error _ => .error "corrupt"
      | .ok j => match Scry.lcFuncOfJson j with
        | .some f => Scry.lcBodyParseGo (List.cons f funcs) warnings externals rest
        | .none => .error "corrupt"
    | .none => match String.stripPrefix line "warning\t" with
      | .some js => match Json.parse js with
        | .error _ => .error "corrupt"
        | .ok j => match Scry.lcWarningOfJson j with
          | .some w => Scry.lcBodyParseGo funcs (List.cons w warnings) externals rest
          | .none => .error "corrupt"
      | .none => match String.stripPrefix line "external\t" with
        | .some js => match Json.parse js with
          | .error _ => .error "corrupt"
          | .ok j => match Scry.lcExternalOfJson j with
            | .some e => Scry.lcBodyParseGo funcs warnings (List.cons e externals) rest
            | .none => .error "corrupt"
        | .none => .error "corrupt"

/-- Check a parsed body against the header counts and the canonical re-rendering. The
body parser has already rejected malformed lines; here a count mismatch or a body that
does not re-render identically is corruption. -/
def Scry.lowerBodyCheck (target : String) (paths texts : List String) (body : String)
    (fc wc ec : Nat) (bodyParsed : Scry.LowerBody) : Except String Scry.TU :=
  let countsOk := Bool.and (Nat.beq (List.length bodyParsed.funcs) fc)
    (Bool.and (Nat.beq (List.length bodyParsed.warnings) wc)
      (Nat.beq (List.length bodyParsed.externals) ec));
  match countsOk with
  | .true =>
    let tu := Scry.TU.mk target (Scry.lowerPadTexts paths texts) paths
      bodyParsed.funcs bodyParsed.warnings bodyParsed.externals;
    match String.eq (Scry.lowerBodyOf tu) body with
    | .true => .ok tu
    | .false => .error "corrupt"
  | .false => .error "corrupt"

/-- The header fields a lowering entry carries into the body check. -/
structure Scry.LowerHeader where
  funcs : Nat
  warnings : Nat
  externals : Nat
  body : String

/-- The entry-format check: the magic line. Another lowering format is a clean `miss`;
anything else is `corrupt`. -/
def Scry.lcMagicOk (magic : String) : Except String Unit :=
  match String.eq magic Scry.lowerCacheMagic with
  | .true => .ok ()
  | .false => match String.startsWith magic "scry-project-lower " with
    | .true => .error "miss"
    | .false => .error "corrupt"

/-- One `prefix value` header field that must equal `value`. A mismatch is a `miss` (the
entry belongs to another state); a malformed line is `corrupt`. -/
def Scry.lcFieldEq (line prefix value : String) : Except String Unit :=
  match String.stripPrefix line prefix with
  | .some v => match String.eq v value with
    | .true => .ok ()
    | .false => .error "miss"
  | .none => .error "corrupt"

/-- One `prefix n` header field. -/
def Scry.lcFieldNat (line prefix : String) : Except String Nat :=
  match String.stripPrefix line prefix with
  | .some v => match String.parseNat v with
    | .some n => .ok n
    | .none => .error "corrupt"
  | .none => .error "corrupt"

/-- The `target` header field: a JSON string that must equal the unit target. -/
def Scry.lcTargetField (line target : String) : Except String Unit :=
  match String.stripPrefix line "target " with
  | .some v => match Json.parse v with
    | .ok (.str s) => match String.eq s target with
      | .true => .ok ()
      | .false => .error "miss"
    | _ => .error "corrupt"
  | .none => .error "corrupt"

/-- The `body <len> <digest>` header field: check the byte length and the 64-bit digest
of the body text (the lines after the header), and return that text. -/
def Scry.lcBodyField (line : String) (rest : List String) : Except String String :=
  match String.stripPrefix line "body " with
  | .some v => match String.splitOn v " " with
    | .cons a (.cons b .nil) =>
      let body := String.intercalate "\n" rest;
      match String.parseNat a with
      | .none => .error "corrupt"
      | .some blen => match String.parseNat b with
        | .none => .error "corrupt"
        | .some bdig =>
          match Nat.beq blen (String.length body) with
          | .false => .error "corrupt"
          | .true => match Nat.beq bdig (Scry.fnv body) with
            | .true => .ok body
            | .false => .error "corrupt"
    | _ => .error "corrupt"
  | .none => .error "corrupt"

/-- Validate a lowering entry's header: the format and lowerer stamps, every key input,
the error count, the three body counts, and the body's length/digest. No state is read
from the body yet; `Scry.lowerCacheOf` parses and re-renders it next. -/
def Scry.lowerHeaderOf (magic lowerLine keyLine cstLine preLine targetLine errLine
    funcsLine warnLine extLine bodyLine : String) (key cst pre target : String)
    (errors : Nat) (rest : List String) : Except String Scry.LowerHeader :=
  match Scry.lcMagicOk magic with
  | .error e => .error e
  | .ok _ =>
    match Scry.lcFieldEq lowerLine "lowerer " Scry.projectLowererVersion with
    | .error e => .error e
    | .ok _ =>
      match Scry.lcFieldEq keyLine "key " key with
      | .error e => .error e
      | .ok _ =>
        match Scry.lcFieldEq cstLine "cst " cst with
        | .error e => .error e
        | .ok _ =>
          match Scry.lcFieldEq preLine "pre " pre with
          | .error e => .error e
          | .ok _ =>
            match Scry.lcTargetField targetLine target with
            | .error e => .error e
            | .ok _ =>
              match Scry.lcFieldEq errLine "errors " (Nat.repr errors) with
              | .error e => .error e
              | .ok _ =>
                match Scry.lcFieldNat funcsLine "funcs " with
                | .error e => .error e
                | .ok fc =>
                  match Scry.lcFieldNat warnLine "warnings " with
                  | .error e => .error e
                  | .ok wc =>
                    match Scry.lcFieldNat extLine "externals " with
                    | .error e => .error e
                    | .ok ec =>
                      match Scry.lcBodyField bodyLine rest with
                      | .error e => .error e
                      | .ok body => .ok (Scry.LowerHeader.mk fc wc ec body)

/-- Parse a lowering entry. Header fields that name other inputs are a `miss` (the entry
belongs to a different state); a malformed line, a truncated body or a body digest that
does not match is `corrupt`. Both are recomputed and rewritten, so neither can be a stale
hit. The body is re-rendered and compared after parsing: a decoder that drops or reorders
a field is corruption, not a silently different TU. -/
def Scry.lowerCacheOf (key cst pre target : String) (errors : Nat)
    (paths texts : List String) (text : String) : Except String Scry.TU :=
  match String.splitOn text "\n" with
  | .cons magic (.cons lowerLine (.cons keyLine (.cons cstLine (.cons preLine
      (.cons targetLine (.cons errLine (.cons funcsLine (.cons warnLine
      (.cons extLine (.cons bodyLine rest)))))))))) =>
    match Scry.lowerHeaderOf magic lowerLine keyLine cstLine preLine targetLine errLine
        funcsLine warnLine extLine bodyLine key cst pre target errors rest with
    | .error e => .error e
    | .ok h =>
      match Scry.lcBodyParseGo .nil .nil .nil
          (List.filter (fun l => Bool.not (String.isEmpty l)) rest) with
      | .error e => .error e
      | .ok parsed => Scry.lowerBodyCheck target paths texts h.body h.funcs h.warnings
          h.externals parsed
  | _ => .error "corrupt"

/-- Read and validate one lowering entry. An absent entry is `cold`, an unreadable one
`corrupt`, and another state's entry `miss`; all three recompute. -/
def Scry.lowerCacheLoad (dir key cst pre target : String) (errors : Nat)
    (paths texts : List String) : EIO (Except String Scry.TU) := do
  let p := Scry.lowerCachePath dir key;
  there ← EIO.lift (FS.exists p);
  match there with
  | .false => EIO.pure (.error "cold")
  | .true => do
    textRes ← EIO.lift (FS.readFile p);
    match textRes with
    | .error _ => EIO.pure (.error "corrupt")
    | .ok text => EIO.pure (Scry.lowerCacheOf key cst pre target errors paths texts text)

/-- The entry text: a fixed header naming every key input and the body's length/digest,
then the body (one canonical JSON record per line). `Scry.lowerCacheOf` requires all of
it to agree before returning a TU. -/
def Scry.lowerEntryText (key cst pre target : String) (errors : Nat) (tu : Scry.TU) : String :=
  let body := Scry.lowerBodyOf tu;
  Scry.lowerCacheMagic ++ "\nlowerer " ++ Scry.projectLowererVersion
    ++ "\nkey " ++ key ++ "\ncst " ++ cst ++ "\npre " ++ pre
    ++ "\ntarget " ++ Json.render (Json.mkStr target)
    ++ "\nerrors " ++ Nat.repr errors
    ++ "\nfuncs " ++ Nat.repr (List.length tu.funcs)
    ++ "\nwarnings " ++ Nat.repr (List.length tu.warnings)
    ++ "\nexternals " ++ Nat.repr (List.length tu.externalCalls)
    ++ "\nbody " ++ Nat.repr (String.length body) ++ " " ++ Nat.repr (Scry.fnv body)
    ++ "\n" ++ body

/-- Write one lowering entry atomically (a sibling `.tmp` and a rename, through the same
`Scry.cacheWriteAtomic` the parse cache uses). -/
def Scry.lowerCacheSave (dir key cst pre target : String) (errors : Nat)
    (tu : Scry.TU) : EIO Unit := do
  Scry.mkdirp dir;
  Scry.cacheWriteAtomic (Scry.lowerCachePath dir key)
    (Scry.lowerEntryText key cst pre target errors tu)

/-- The human cache line (`cache: hit=… miss=… …`). -/
def Scry.CacheStats.line (s : Scry.CacheStats) : String :=
  match s.enabled with
  | .false => "cache: disabled"
  | .true => "cache: hit=" ++ Nat.repr s.hit ++ " miss=" ++ Nat.repr s.miss
      ++ " write=" ++ Nat.repr s.write ++ " corrupt=" ++ Nat.repr s.corrupt
      ++ " files=" ++ Nat.repr s.files ++ " reason=" ++ s.reason
      ++ " dir=" ++ s.dir

/-- The JSON object for `--json`/`--cache-stats`. -/
def Scry.CacheStats.json (s : Scry.CacheStats) : Json :=
  Json.mkObj
    [ ("enabled", Json.mkBool s.enabled)
    , ("dir", Json.mkStr s.dir)
    , ("hit", Json.ofNat s.hit)
    , ("miss", Json.ofNat s.miss)
    , ("write", Json.ofNat s.write)
    , ("corrupt", Json.ofNat s.corrupt)
    , ("files", Json.ofNat s.files)
    , ("reason", Json.mkStr s.reason) ]

-- ## The findings index (after a `--taint` run)

/-- What the last findings-index check did. `reason` is `hit`, `cold`, `corrupt` or
`disabled`; `bySeverity` is the in-memory count a query must reproduce. -/
structure Scry.FindingsCacheStats where
  backend : String
  reason : String
  rows : Nat
  bySeverity : List (Nat × Nat)

/-- The human findings-index line (`findings index: backend=quire reason=hit rows=2 …`). -/
def Scry.FindingsCacheStats.line (s : Scry.FindingsCacheStats) : String :=
  "findings index: backend=" ++ s.backend ++ " reason=" ++ s.reason
    ++ " rows=" ++ Nat.repr s.rows
    ++ " bySeverity=" ++ String.intercalate ","
      (List.map (fun p => Nat.repr p.2) s.bySeverity)

/-- `<dir>/findings-<id>.<ext>`: one index per project id and backend, so a backend
switch never reads the other format. -/
def Scry.findingsCachePath (cache : Scry.CacheOpts) (id : String) : String :=
  String.append cache.dir (String.append "/findings-"
    (String.append id (String.append "." (Scry.CacheBackend.ext cache.backend))))

/-- Read the findings index. `error "cold"` when absent, `error "corrupt"` for a
truncated, garbage or stale document; neither is a failure of the run. -/
def Scry.findingsLoad (cache : Scry.CacheOpts) (id : String) :
    EIO (Except String (List Scry.FindingKey)) :=
  let p := Scry.findingsCachePath cache id;
  match cache.backend with
  | .json => do
    loaded ← Scry.fiLoadJson p;
    match loaded with
    | .error e => EIO.pure (.error e)
    | .ok rows => EIO.pure (.ok (List.map Scry.FindingsIndexRow.key rows))
  | .quire => Scry.fiLoadQuire p

/-- Write the findings index. -/
def Scry.findingsSave (cache : Scry.CacheOpts) (id : String)
    (rows : List Scry.FindingsIndexRow) : EIO Unit :=
  let p := Scry.findingsCachePath cache id;
  match cache.backend with
  | .json => Scry.fiSaveJson p rows
  | .quire => Scry.fiSaveQuire p rows

/-- The warm read: `(rows, fingerprint)`. Quire reads the meta and two sentinel records
(a warm run is O(1), not a page walk); JSON parses the document, which is the format's
cost. A missing, truncated or garbage file is a clean `error`. -/
def Scry.findingsProbe (cache : Scry.CacheOpts) (id : String) :
    EIO (Except String (Nat × U64)) :=
  let p := Scry.findingsCachePath cache id;
  match cache.backend with
  | .json => do
    loaded ← Scry.fiLoadJson p;
    match loaded with
    | .error e => EIO.pure (.error e)
    | .ok rows =>
      let ks := List.map Scry.FindingsIndexRow.key rows;
      EIO.pure (.ok (List.length ks, Scry.fiPrint ks))
  | .quire => Scry.fiProbe p

/-- The index hit: how many findings have `severity`. `quire` answers through the
severity index (no primary read); `json` parses and filters the document. -/
def Scry.findingsQuery (cache : Scry.CacheOpts) (id : String) (severity : Nat) :
    EIO (Except String Nat) :=
  let p := Scry.findingsCachePath cache id;
  match cache.backend with
  | .json => Scry.fiCountSeverityJson p severity
  | .quire => Scry.fiCountSeverityQuire p (U32.ofNat severity)

/-- The severity counts of `rows`: the numbers a query must reproduce. -/
def Scry.severityCounts (rows : List Scry.FindingsIndexRow) : List (Nat × Nat) :=
  List.map (fun n => (n, List.length (List.filter (fun r => Nat.beq r.severity n) rows)))
    [0, 1, 2]

/-- Check the findings index for this run, and write it on a cold or stale entry. The
report is never read from the index: the index is a derived projection, so an index miss
cannot change what the analysis reports. A hit requires the same row count and
fingerprint; a stale or corrupt entry is rewritten. -/
def Scry.cacheFindings (cache : Scry.CacheOpts) (id : String)
    (rows0 : List Scry.FindingsIndexRow) : EIO Scry.FindingsCacheStats :=
  let rows := Scry.fiDedupe rows0;
  let want := List.map Scry.FindingsIndexRow.key rows;
  let stats := Scry.FindingsCacheStats.mk (Scry.CacheBackend.show cache.backend) ""
    (List.length rows) (Scry.severityCounts rows);
  match cache.enabled with
  | .false => EIO.pure { stats with reason := "disabled" }
  | .true => do
    loaded ← Scry.findingsProbe cache id;
    let reason := match loaded with
      | .ok pr => match Bool.and (Nat.beq pr.1 (List.length want))
            (UIntN.beq pr.2 (Scry.fiPrint want)) with
        | .true => "hit"
        | .false => "corrupt"
      | .error e => e;
    match String.eq reason "hit" with
    | .true => EIO.pure { stats with reason := reason }
    | .false => do
      Scry.mkdirp cache.dir;
      Scry.findingsSave cache id rows;
      EIO.pure { stats with reason := reason }
